# 4. Priority P1 — Introduce a Personal Reading Shelf

This is the major new functionality for this phase.

Build a polished, privacy-first **Reading Shelf** system.

The Reading Shelf allows visitors to save articles and manage their personal reading list directly in the browser.

This is not a CMS, account system, or backend feature.

## 4.1 Article bookmarking

Introduce a reusable Save Article / Bookmark interaction.

Users should be able to save or remove an article from:

- Individual article pages.
- Featured article cards.
- Homepage article cards and compact article rows wherever the existing component architecture supports it efficiently.

The button must have clearly distinguishable states:

- Not saved.
- Saved.
- Hovered.
- Focused.
- Disabled or unavailable, if applicable.

Suggested interaction:

- Unsaved: bookmark outline icon.
- Saved: filled bookmark icon with restrained primary accent.
- On successful save: subtle visual feedback and a toast.
- On removal: immediate UI state update.

Do not rely exclusively on color to communicate state.

Use accessible labels and keyboard interaction.

Avoid excessive client-side hydration across large article lists. Use the lightest reusable implementation that fits the existing Astro and React architecture.

## 4.2 Local persistence

Create a dedicated saved-articles store following the existing Nanostores and local-storage conventions.

Suggested architecture:

- A dedicated store such as `src/store/saved-articles.ts`.
- Reusable UI component such as `SaveArticleButton.tsx`.
- Appropriate library helpers and tests.
- Versioned local-storage schema.

Each saved item should store only the information needed to display and open the article:

- Article slug.
- Article title.
- Article URL.
- Locale.
- Save timestamp.
- Category, when available.
- Short description, when available.
- Cover image path, when available.

Requirements:

- Persist saved items across browser sessions.
- Validate stored data before using it.
- Handle malformed or outdated stored data safely.
- Prevent duplicate saved entries.
- Distinguish localized article URLs correctly.
- Support removing individual entries.
- Support clearing the entire shelf with confirmation.
- Handle local-storage failures gracefully.
- Keep saved data synchronized with the UI.
- Use existing storage patterns where possible.

Never send saved-article data to an external server.

Do not add user tracking, analytics, authentication, or third-party storage.

## 4.3 Dedicated Reading Shelf page

Create a polished, localized Reading Shelf page using the project's existing Astro route and layout conventions.

The page should feel like a personal digital library, not a generic admin dashboard.

Suggested visual structure:

**Header area**
- Editorial kicker: YOUR LIBRARY.
- Large title: Reading Shelf.
- Short descriptive subtitle.
- Saved article count.
- Small botanical or celestial ornament.

**Reading status navigation**

Provide four useful filtering states:

- All Saved.
- To Read.
- In Progress.
- Completed.

Derive reading status from the saved-article data and the existing reading-history store.

Do not create conflicting or duplicate reading-progress records.

**Saved article collection**

Each entry should display:

- Cover image or graceful fallback.
- Article title.
- Category.
- Original publication date, if available.
- Estimated reading time.
- Save date.
- Reading progress, when available.
- Current reading status.
- Open / Continue Reading action.
- Remove from shelf action.

Support:

- Search within saved articles.
- Sorting by save date and title.
- Responsive grid or editorial list layouts.
- Empty states.
- Clear-all interaction with confirmation.

Use the existing article metadata and reading-history data wherever possible.

Do not invent article statistics or fake content.

## 4.4 Integrate Reading Shelf into existing navigation

Make the feature easy to discover without overcrowding the interface.

Provide entry points through:

- Homepage utility rail.
- Compact homepage utility area on tablet/mobile.
- Command Palette.
- Article reading toolbar.
- Existing navigation or menu, if appropriate.

A compact shelf widget on the homepage may show the saved count and the two most recently saved articles.

Do not add a large redundant homepage section just to advertise this feature.

## 4.5 Reading Shelf visual states

Design complete states:

- First-time empty shelf.
- Saved articles available.
- No search results.
- Filter with no matches.
- Loading or hydration transition.
- Storage unavailable.
- Item successfully removed.

Empty states should be visually attractive and useful, with links to explore existing content.

